# ###################################################################
# Exhibitions / Zones
# ###################################################################

# Zone 1: Post-War Boom
<http://radio.liv.ac.uk/bme/Exhibition/Zone1> a scho:ExhibitionEvent ;
    scho:name "Post-War Boom" ;
    scho:startDate "1945"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:endDate "1962"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:description "The Second World War cemented not only an Anglo-American special relationship in politics but in the evolution of musical trends in Britain. American Gls reinforced the popularity of big band music - which filtered onto BBC radio programmes - and the revived sounds of Dixieland and New Orleans-inspired trad jazz became the live sound on the London club scene. An equally popular genre of the 1950s was skiffle, a folk-blues revival launched by Lonnie Donegan. Its do-it-yourself creed and musical simplicity allowed a new generation of young musicians to pick up cheap guitars, washboards and tea chest basses and start their own skiffle groups. From the mid-1950s, British pop was transformed by the rock 'n' roll craze. Inspired by the likes of Bill Haley, Elvis Presley, Chuck Berry and Little Richard, British rock 'n' rollers sprung up everywhere as teenagers found a new musical, social and economic freedom."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Exhibition: Post-War Boom (1945-1962)"@en ;
    scho:image <http://museum.example.org/images/zone1.jpg> .

# Zone 2: Swinging London
<http://radio.liv.ac.uk/bme/Exhibition/Zone2> a scho:ExhibitionEvent ;
    scho:name "Swinging London" ;
    scho:startDate "1962"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:endDate "1966"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:description "In the 1960s, British music went global for the first time with the irresistible tide of the 'British Invasion'. The original look and sound of Beat groups like The Beatles, R&B bands like The Rolling Stones and female singers like Petula Clark took America by storm. Signalled by mass fan hysteria and huge record sales, Britain became the pop powerhouse. At this time, music, fashion and youth culture was moving optimistically into a period of intense stylistic and technological change, as the British economy recovered after post-War rationing and austerity. Skirts became shorter Quant-style, music more diverse and bold colours and patterns filtered into 60's design thanks to the success of Pop Art. In April 1966, Time magazine published their report on the world's most fashionable city,'Swinging London'."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Exhibition: Swinging London (1962-1966)"@en ;
    scho:image <http://museum.example.org/images/zone2.jpg> .

# Zone 3: Cultural Revolution
<http://radio.liv.ac.uk/bme/Exhibition/Zone3> a scho:ExhibitionEvent ;
    scho:name "Cultural Revolution" ;
    scho:startDate "1966"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:endDate "1970"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:description "From mid-1966, British pop began to change as it became increasingly involved in cultural exchanges with underground political activity, fashion, art and drugs. The psychedelic period found its peak during the 1967 'Summer of Love'. Inspired by the mass gathering of hippies in San Francisco, Britain staged their own 'love-ins' in London. Drug experimentation with themes of peace and sexual freedom became both the catalyst and subject for music, capturing the essence of this period. The success of The Beatles' 1967 Sgt. Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band LP signalled the shift from the 7\" single to the 12\" album. Rock developed as a genre and there was greater experimentation with sounds and styles from around the world, which were showcased on the emerging festival scene."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Exhibition: Cultural Revolution (1966-1970)"@en ;
    scho:image <http://museum.example.org/images/zone3.jpg> .

# Zone 4: Glam Rock & Rock Spectacle
<http://radio.liv.ac.uk/bme/Exhibition/Zone4> a scho:ExhibitionEvent ;
    scho:name "Glam Rock & Rock Spectacle" ;
    scho:startDate "1970"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:endDate "1975"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:description "In the early 1970s, glam emerged in direct contrast to the tough economic crisis Britain was suffering due to extreme strike action by trade unions and spiralling inflation. The charts were soon full of performers singing of escapism, glamour and excitement. Extravagance was the order of the day, as well as a renewed concentration on teenage lyrics, which reflected the concerns of a new pop generation. Bisexuality and androgyny were explored as fashions became more outlandish, make-up was applied and platforms were donned. Some artists, such as David Bowie, preferred to voice a darker prospect of 70s Britain with his post-apocalyptic Diamond Dogs album. This renewed sensationalism transferred to the elaborate rock staging and rock theatre in huge arenas, as the music industry expanded and audiences sought entertainment rather than political engagement."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Exhibition: Glam Rock & Rock Spectacle (1970-1975)"@en ;
    scho:image <http://museum.example.org/images/zone4.jpg> .

# Zone 5: Punk & New Romantics
<http://radio.liv.ac.uk/bme/Exhibition/Zone5> a scho:ExhibitionEvent ;
    scho:name "Punk & New Romantics" ;
    scho:startDate "1975"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:endDate "1985"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:description "The mid to late 1970s was a period of economic recession and unemployment in Britain. Pop music reflected these conditions with tougher, more socially-engaged styles in reaction to the frivolity and camp of glam. Punk luridly dramatised Britain's social divisions, while the grassroots Rock Against Racism movement popularised reggae and brought political involvement back to pop. As a back-to-basics movement, punk also inspired many new styles, including the ska revival of 2-Tone. In the early 1980s, the impact of Margaret Thatcher's premiership became apparent, and music reacted to the emergent social and political events. There was a move back to glamour and fashion with the brief, but influential, 'New Romantic' style and the increased popularity of black-derived electro and jazz funk dance music."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Exhibition: Punk & New Romantics (1975-1985)"@en ;
    scho:image <http://museum.example.org/images/zone5.jpg> .

# Zone 6: Madchester & Live Aid
<http://radio.liv.ac.uk/bme/Exhibition/Zone6> a scho:ExhibitionEvent ;
    scho:name "Madchester & Live Aid" ;
    scho:startDate "1985"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:endDate "1993"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:description "The mid-to-late 1980s was a time of accelerated social, economic, technical and political change. Out of the union struggles and privatisation talks emerged yuppies riding the economic wave. New developments in technology meant that all media was more easily accessible through video, CD and satellite broadcasting. In July 1985, the Live Aid concert brought performances by the best musicians in the world to an estimated 400 million people in 60 countries to raise money for famine-stricken Ethiopia. The late 1980s were also a time of regional and musical diversity. Heavy metal was reborn, imported house music reached ecstatic heights with the 'Second Summer of Love' and the 'Madchester' scene was developing in the North."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Exhibition: Madchester & Live Aid (1985-1993)"@en ;
    scho:image <http://museum.example.org/images/zone6.jpg> .

# Zone 7: Britpop & Girl Power
<http://radio.liv.ac.uk/bme/Exhibition/Zone7> a scho:ExhibitionEvent ;
    scho:name "Britpop & Girl Power" ;
    scho:startDate "1993"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:endDate "2004"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:description "In the mid-1990s, 'Cool Britannia' swept through all areas of British identity. New Labour offered political change, Saatchi's Young British Artists shocked the art world and came to the fore with its return to guitar-based songs. The period also saw the rise of the manufactured boy and girl band, with Take That and Boyzone winning the hearts of many a young girl and Girl Power bursting onto the scene with the Spice Girls. For the first time since The Beatles, fan-mania swept the country and pop memorabilia hit stores nationwide, marketed at young teens. The new millennium was a celebration of something quintessentially British with echoes of past styles updated for a new generation. But change was looming in the music industry. The rise of the digital music revolution meant relationships between artists, record companies, technology and fans were set to launch the music industry into a great depression as piracy fears prevailed and sales plunged."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Exhibition: Britpop & Girl Power (1993-2004)"@en ;
    scho:image <http://museum.example.org/images/zone7.jpg> .

# Zone 8: The New Millennium
<http://radio.liv.ac.uk/bme/Exhibition/Zone8> a scho:ExhibitionEvent ;
    scho:name "The New Millennium" ;
    scho:startDate "2004"^^xsd:gYear ;
    scho:description "The beginning of the 21st Century ushered in an incredibly turbulent period witnessing a major financial crisis, wars in the Middle-East and Ukraine, a global Pandemic and widespread political instability. The music industry underwent one of its most transformative periods yet, moving from the dawn of downloads to the age of streaming, with services like Spotify making their first contributions to the Official Singles Chart in 2014, and becoming one of the defining measures of an artist's popularity. This shift online made the music industry a more accessible and diverse environment, with grassroots genres like Grime enjoying huge success, with Stormzy headlining Glastonbury festival in 2019. This embracing of online culture, compounded by Covid-19, saw artists utilising every tool at their disposal, notably services like TikTok, to build fanbases and promote their music to wider audiences. While TV Talent Shows like The X Factor came and went during this time, some of the artists involved went on to stratospheric success, with acts like Little Mix becoming one of Britain's biggest selling acts of all time, with over 60 million records sold worldwide and Harry Styles becoming a global superstar, eclipsing even his former band One Direction and their astonishing successes. And throughout it all the annual BRIT Awards continue to prevail as a symbol of success. Celebrating the very best of British Popular Music, The BRITs reach ever higher viewing figures with millions tuning in globally every year, maintaining Britain's position as one of THE international powerhouses of pop music."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Exhibition: The New Millennium (2004-Present)"@en ;
    scho:image <http://museum.example.org/images/zone8.jpg> .

